    "extraction_notes": "Vector table with a clean text layer. Rule-line geometry recovered from a 200 dpi raster render gives 13 vertical rules (1 label column + 11 timepoint columns) and only three horizontal rules (table top, below the header row, table bottom), i.e. exactly one body row. Marks binned to the timepoint columns from pdftotext -bbox token centres and diffed against a visual read. One leading label column (L=1); first data column is position 2."
  },
  "table_metadata": {
    "table_id": "table-002",
    "table_number": 2,
    "table_type": "subsidiary",
    "table_title": "Schedule of Pharmacokinetic and Pharmacodynamic Assessments on Days 1, 25, 29, and 39",
    "table_purpose": "Hour-by-hour PK/PD blood sampling timepoints on Days 1, 25, 29 and 39 for the PK and PD sampling activities that appear as single rows in Table 1.",
    "page_start": 17,
    "page_end": 17,
    "track_label": null,
    "column_count": 11,
    "row_count": 3,
    "header_row_count": 1,
    "activity_row_count": 2,
    "notes": "Classified 'subsidiary': the columns are a finer timing grid (hours post-dose) than Table 1's day columns, and the table gives detailed timing for a subset of Table 1's activities - Table 1 footnote o says 'See Table 2 for PK/PD sampling on Days 1, 25, 29, and 39' and footnote p points at the same table. It is not a track (same participants, same treatment period) and not a reference table (its rows are assessments performed on participants). STRUCTURE NOTE: the source prints ONE rule-bounded body cell whose text is four lines naming TWO assessments ('Blood sampling for PK: Plasma total Mo and PUF-Mo' and 'PD: Plasma total and PUF-Cu, LBC, ceruloplasmin, ceruloplasmin-bound Cu'), with a single row of marks vertically centred across all four lines. The two assessments are separate activity rows in Table 1, so they are emitted here as two activities (row_position 2 and 3) and the centred marks are applied to both, per the vertically-merged-mark convention. No horizontal rule separates the two names - that is an extraction decision, not a printed row split."
  },
